      "problem": "Rewrite $-9{z}^{2}-6z+9$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-9.$ So, we take out a factor of $-9$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1) & =-9({(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot (-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$",
